## DataFrame & Dataset API

### DataFrames

DataFrame is distributed collection of rows organized into named columns (like relational table or pandas DataFrame).

**Advantages over RDD:**
- Easier to use (SQL-like syntax)
- Better optimization (Catalyst optimizer)
- Better performance
- Column types known upfront

### Creating DataFrames

**From Collections:**
```python
from pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, IntegerType, StringType

# From Python dict list
data = [
    {"name": "John", "age": 30, "salary": 50000},
    {"name": "Jane", "age": 28, "salary": 60000},
    {"name": "Bob", "age": 35, "salary": 55000}
]
df = spark.createDataFrame(data)

# From tuples with schema
data = [("John", 30), ("Jane", 28), ("Bob", 35)]
schema = StructType([
    StructField("name", StringType(), True),
    StructField("age", IntegerType(), True)
])
df = spark.createDataFrame(data, schema=schema)

# Inferred schema (slower)
df = spark.createDataFrame(data).toDF("name", "age")
```

**From External Data:**
```python
# From CSV
df = spark.read.csv("path/to/file.csv", header=True, inferSchema=True)

# From Parquet
df = spark.read.parquet("path/to/file.parquet")

# From JSON
df = spark.read.json("path/to/file.json")

# From JDBC (Database)
df = spark.read \
    .format("jdbc") \
    .option("url", "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db") \
    .option("dbtable", "employees") \
    .option("user", "user") \
    .option("password", "password") \
    .load()

# From Hive Table
df = spark.sql("SELECT * FROM hive_table")
```

**From RDD:**
```python
# RDD to DataFrame
rdd = sc.parallelize([("John", 30), ("Jane", 28)])
df = rdd.toDF(["name", "age"])
```

### DataFrame Operations

**Basic Operations:**
```python
df = spark.createDataFrame([
    ("John", 30, 50000),
    ("Jane", 28, 60000),
    ("Bob", 35, 55000)
], ["name", "age", "salary"])

# Show data
df.show(truncate=False)
df.display()  # In Databricks

# Get schema
df.printSchema()
df.schema

# Describe (statistics)
df.describe().show()
df.describe(["age", "salary"]).show()

# Count rows
df.count()

# Get number of columns
len(df.columns)

# Get column names
df.columns
```

**Selection & Filtering:**
```python
# Select columns
df.select("name", "age").show()
df.select([col for col in df.columns if col != "salary"]).show()

# Select with expression
from pyspark.sql.functions import col, expr
df.select(col("name"), (col("salary") / 12).alias("monthly_salary")).show()
df.selectExpr("name", "salary / 12 as monthly_salary").show()

# Where clause (filtering)
df.where(col("age") > 30).show()
df.where("salary > 55000").show()
df.filter(col("age") > 30).show()

# Using SQL expressions
df.where((col("age") > 25) & (col("salary") > 50000)).show()
df.where((col("age") > 25) | (col("salary") > 55000)).show()
```

**Sorting & Limiting:**
```python
# Sort
df.sort("age").show()  # Ascending
df.sort(col("salary").desc()).show()  # Descending
df.orderBy("age", col("salary").desc()).show()  # Multiple

# Limit
df.limit(2).show()

# Take (like collect)
df.take(2)
```

**Grouping & Aggregation:**
```python
# GroupBy with aggregation
df.groupBy("age").count().show()
df.groupBy("age").agg({"salary": "avg"}).show()

# Multiple aggregations
from pyspark.sql.functions import avg, sum as spark_sum, max as spark_max
df.groupBy("age").agg(
    avg("salary").alias("avg_salary"),
    spark_sum("salary").alias("total_salary"),
    spark_max("salary").alias("max_salary")
).show()

# Multiple group columns
df.groupBy("age", "name").count().show()
```

