## Caching & Optimization

### Caching

Caching stores data in memory for reuse.

```python
# Cache in memory
df.cache()
df.persist()  # Equivalent to cache()

# Cache with specific storage level
from pyspark import StorageLevel
df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY)
df.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)
df.persist(StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY)

# Check if cached
df.is_cached

# Remove from cache
df.unpersist()

# Example: Cache for repeated use
df = spark.read.csv("large_file.csv")
df.cache()
df.count()  # Loads into cache

# Subsequent operations use cache
df.filter(col("age") > 30).show()  # Fast
df.groupBy("department").count().show()  # Fast
df.select("name", "salary").show()  # Fast

df.unpersist()  # Free memory
```

### Optimization Techniques

**Predicate Pushdown:**
```python
# Good: Filter pushed to storage layer
df = spark.read.parquet("large_file.parquet")
result = df.filter(col("date") > "2024-01-01").select("name", "salary")

# Spark pushes filter to storage layer
# Only reads filtered data
```

**Column Pruning:**
```python
# Good: Only reads needed columns
df = spark.read.parquet("large_file.parquet")
result = df.select("name", "salary")  # Only these columns read

# Bad: Reads all columns
result = df  # All columns in memory
```

**Broadcast Variables:**
```python
# For small tables in joins
from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast

small_df = spark.createDataFrame([...], ...)
large_df = spark.createDataFrame([...], ...)

# Broadcast small_df
result = large_df.join(broadcast(small_df), on="id")

# Avoids shuffle, more efficient
```

**Repartitioning for Performance:**
```python
# Too many small partitions: High overhead
df = df.repartition(1000)

# Too few large partitions: Low parallelism
df = df.repartition(2)

# Optimal: 100-200MB per partition
df = df.repartition(50)

# Repartition by column for grouping
df = df.repartition(col("date"))

# Coalesce (reduces partitions without shuffle)
df = df.coalesce(10)
```

